Text Analyzer Project / Salih Egbaria

Extensions:

Processes text by transforming it to lowercase, removing punctuation, and cleaning spaces.

Utilizes utility functions from utils.py, such as convert_lowercase(), remove_punctuation(),
and remove_redundant_spaces() to ensure clean and uniform text analysis.

Offers improved readability and data consistency, making the analysis more effective.


Design

The project structure was crafted with flexibility and efficiency in mind:

Modular Architecture:

Every task is split into individual modules (TASK1 to TASK9) is isolated in its own module
 (task1.py, task2.py, etc.) to promote maintainability and scalability.

The main.py script serves as the central command interface, allowing users to specify tasks and input files via
 command-line arguments.

This structure facilitates easy updates and debugging for individual modules without affecting the entire project.

Utility Functions:

Shared functions in utils.py prevent code duplication and streamline common operations like text cleaning and formatting.

They ensure consistency across all modules and simplify future enhancements.

This design ensures robust, efficient text analysis while embracing creative constraints for specialized operations,
balancing theoretical exploration with practical implementation.


Algorithms Used

I used several algorithms in the working of this project:

Text Preprocessing Algorithms:

Lowercasing Algorithm: Converts all text to lowercase for uniformity.

Punctuation Removal Algorithm: Replaces punctuation with spaces to clean the text.

Whitespace Normalization: Removes redundant spaces using iterative replacements.

Validation Algorithms:

File Path Validation: Ensures that the provided file paths are valid CSV or JSON files.

Task Number Validation: verifies if the given task number falls within a valid range.
Sequence Handling Algorithms:

Tokenization: Splits text into tokens for sequence analysis.

Sliding Window Algorithm: Used for detecting co-occurrences within a given text window.

Graph-Based Algorithms:
Depth-First Search (DFS): For fixed-length path finding in entity relationships.
Cycle Detection Algorithm: Identifies cycles within graphs to prevent infinite loops during traversal.

Why I Have Chosen This Approach

Graph-Based Methods:

Optimizes performing analyses of text relationships to ensure that performance remains efficient for large datasets.
Improves the capability for the detection and analysis of complex relations between entities.
Algorithm Selection:

DFS (Depth-First Search) was chosen for its effectiveness in solving path-related problems.

These algorithms ensure accurate and efficient traversal for different connection scenarios.






